theorem boundary, Lp APIs, and handoff/verification discipline in this checkout; recheck current manuscript locators, mathlib version, and open obligations before claiming progress\n\n## Task 1: identify the live theorem boundary and establish the approved workline\n\n### rollout_summary_files\n\n- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T20-29-40-U10h-countable_pauli_foundation_handoff_reviewed.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T22-29-40-019fc42a-ffcc-7d22-8496-0a357974f5e5.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T05:32:06+00:00, thread_id=019fc42a-ffcc-7d22-8496-0a357974f5e5, partial: approved workline and precise theorem boundary)\n\n### keywords\n\n- thm:finite_projections_exist, full_tracial_corner_semifinite, finite fibers, central projection, qMq, fullness, formalization_finite_fibers, VNTrace\n\n## Task 2: build and review the countable configuration-space L² Pauli foundation\n\n### rollout_summary_files\n\n- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T20-29-40-U10h-countable_pauli_foundation_handoff_reviewed.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T22-29-40-019fc42a-ffcc-7d22-8496-0a357974f5e5.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T05:32:06+00:00, thread_id=019fc42a-ffcc-7d22-8496-0a357974f5e5, partial: checked L² layer; global theorem explicitly open)\n\n### keywords\n\n- CountablePauliRepresentation.lean, CountablePauliConfiguration, Lp, Measure.count, configurationShift, configurationPhase, MeasureTheory.Lp.compMeasurePreservingₗᵢ, ContinuousLinearMap.holderL, AxiomAudit.lean, HANDOFF.md\n\n## User preferences\n\n- when the user said “don‘t start, lets discuss it first” -> discuss the theorem scope and plan before creating the formalization workline or editing artifacts [Task 1]\n- after approving the workline, the user requested adversarial subagents checking whether “the theorem statement is a faithful representation of the mathematics” -> obtain independent mathematical-fidelity and Lean/API review for substantive formalization artifacts [Task 1][Task 2]\n- when the user requested a “handsoff file” and current-status documentation -> leave durable resume artifacts with exact checked/open boundaries rather than relying on chat context [Task 2]\n\n## Reusable knowledge\n\n- The live theorem is `writing/overleaf_prl_stabilizer_region_algebras/main.tex:1501` (`thm:finite_projections_exist`): every nonzero central projection `z` has a finite nonzero projection in `zM_Γ`. The stronger reusable target is `full_tracial_corner_semifinite`: a faithful tracial corner `qMq` plus full `q` implies each nonzero central summand contains a finite nonzero projection; finite central fibers/sectors are a corollary, not finite-dimensional fibers. [Task 1]\n- Use the standalone `formalization_finite_fibers/` package, preserving `work/OLD/formalization/`. Its old `VNTrace` interface is too weak for complex-linear trace subtraction such as `τ(q - vv*) = 0`. [Task 1]\n- `FiniteFiber/CountablePauliRepresentation.lean` realizes `K := ℕ →₀ ZMod 2` and `H := Lp ℂ 2 (Measure.count)`, with translations and bounded phase multipliers. Lp action formulas are almost-everywhere (`=ᵐ[Measure.count]`): translations use `MeasureTheory.measurePreserving_add_left` and `MeasureTheory.Lp.compMeasurePreservingₗᵢ`; phase multiplication uses an L∞ certificate and `ContinuousLinearMap.holderL`. [Task 2]\n- Verify with focused and full `lake build`, `lake env lean tests/AxiomAudit.lean`, direct theorem audits, and the handoff. Checked declarations used only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`; both independent reviews were APPROVED. [Task 2]\n- The next local step is shift composition, then involution/operator Pauli relations; the larger bridge remains finite/tail factorization and faithful finite local matrix images. [Task 2]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- symptom: a formalization tracks an earlier short-range theorem or calls finite fibers finite-dimensional -> cause: the live proof spine and terminology were not audited -> fix: begin at `thm:finite_projections_exist` and distinguish central factor sectors from finite-dimensional fibers. [Task 1]\n- symptom: temporary shift-composition proof stalls at a composed continuous-linear-map RHS -> fix: normalize with `ContinuousLinearMap.comp_apply`; do not promote the MWE until it compiles. [Task 2]\n- symptom: the L² foundation is described as operator Pauli relations, finite/tail factorization, quasilocal closure, global state/GNS, corner trace/fullness, or the Overleaf theorem -> fix: retain these as explicit open obligations. [Task 2]\n\n# Task Group: lean/stabilizer standalone Lean tensor-product and trace formalization\n\nscope: separate, approval-gated Lean audit/formalization of the proposed stabilizer-region tensor-product theorem, including checked algebraic transport and an explicit analytic-theorem stop boundary\napplies_to: cwd=lean/stabilizer (source context=source-workspace); reuse_rule=reuse the checked algebraic modules and analytic-obligation checklist only after rechecking pinned mathlib and source assumptions\n\n--- cli reference ---\n# Aristotle CLI reference\n\n## Setup\n\n- Install: `uv tool install aristotlelib`\n- Verify: `aristotle --version`\n- Authenticate: set `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` in the shell environment, or use the skill wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\nIf the key is stored near the end of `~/.bashrc`, noninteractive commands may not see it because many `.bashrc` files contain:\n\n```bash\ncase $- in\n    *i*) ;;\n      *) return;;\nesac\n```\n\nIn that case, do not rely on `source ~/.bashrc`. Use the wrapper, which reads a literal `export ARISTOTLE_API_KEY=...` assignment without printing it:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize input.md\n```\n\n## Core commands\n\nSubmit a Lean project for sorry filling or proof search:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh submit \"Fill in all sorries\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\nFormalize a TeX or paper source:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize paper.tex --wait --destination output.tar.gz\n```\n\nList recent jobs:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh list --limit 10\n```\n\nDownload a completed job:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh download <project-id> --destination result.tar.gz\n```\n\nPass the key directly for one command only:\n\n```bash\naristotle submit \"My prompt\" --api-key \"$ARISTOTLE_API_KEY\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\n## Recommended project loop\n\n1. Inspect the Lean project locally.\n2. Run the narrowest local build or test that exercises the target files.\n3. Submit a focused Aristotle prompt against the project directory.\n4. Wait for the run if the expected result is a concrete patch.\n5. Download or inspect the produced artifact.\n6. Rebuild locally and repeat with a tighter prompt if needed.\n\n## Prompt shape\n\nKeep prompts short and file-bound. Good prompts mention:\n\n- the exact project directory\n- the theorem or file to work on\n- whether to fill `sorry`s, formalize a statement, or debug a failing proof\n- what counts as a useful partial result\n\nAvoid vague prompts such as \"improve this project\" unless the scope is intentionally broad.\n\n--- local guidance files ---\n./AGENTS.md\n./README.md\n./HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02-extracted/project_aristotle/AGENTS.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02